  "corrected": {
    "reason": "Bridge decks slope between the rendered road surfaces at their landing nodes (ends with no road keep the landed height), lean with the mean landing cross slope, and railings/lane lines/dashes/piers/trusses stand on that face. Before, a level slab at the higher bank left 0.1–1.1 steps and no bridge connected at both ends. Every other system, curbs included, must still equal `plans`. Expressway ramp ends (2026-10-16) land on the rendered road surface (deck top = rendered terrain + 0.04 where the end lies on a ground road) instead of terrain.height + 0.3, which left a 0.24–0.26 step at all 22 ramp ends; the half-ramp is shifted and the shift fades out with the same ease. Ramp-zone segments (within 90 of an open end) are drawn as ≤ 10-unit collinear pieces so the eased profile is actually sampled: the 137–280 long single-segment spurs were one straight slab meeting the ring 0.6–1.1 below its deck at a ~30° merge; they now level out before the merge, and long spurs get piers by the same 34-unit mileage rule.",
